      "page_idx": 19,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.604,
            0.044,
            0.848,
            0.061
          ],
          "content": "Describing Motion Around Us"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "67"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.776,
            0.258
          ],
          "content": "Imagine an athlete running along a closed path. In Fig. 4.23a, the athlete follows a rectangular track (ABCD), running at a uniform speed on the straight sections (AB, BC, CD and DA). The athlete changes direction four times to complete one round. In Fig. 4.23b, the athlete is running along a hexagonal path. In this case, the athlete changes direction six times to complete one round. What happens if we go on increasing the number of sides indefinitely? As the number of sides increases, the athlete has to take turns more and more frequently. Finally, the track approaches a circle, with each side decreasing to a point and the direction of athlete’s velocity changes continuously."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.266,
            0.422,
            0.284
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 4.5: Let us investigate"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.287,
            0.674,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": "1. Take a ring, such as an adhesive tape ring and one marble."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.307,
            0.775,
            0.36
          ],
          "content": "2. Place the ring flat on a smooth surface and throw the marble inside the ring in a way that it rotates along the inner boundary of the ring (Fig. 4.24)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.361,
            0.775,
            0.397
          ],
          "content": "3. **Predict** what will happen if you lift the ring while the marble is moving."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.4,
            0.775,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": "4. Now, after one or two complete revolutions of the marble, pick up the ring without disturbing the motion of the marble. What do you observe? Does the marble continue moving in a circular motion? Or does it move in some other manner?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.471,
            0.645,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "5. Repeat the activity multiple times to confirm the result."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.491,
            0.774,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": "When the marble is released by lifting the ring, it moves in a straight line. Why does this happen?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.525,
            0.774,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": "This is because once the marble is released, it continues to move in the direction it has been moving at the instant the ring was removed. You will learn the reason for this in a later chapter."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.577,
            0.155,
            0.633
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.587,
            0.37,
            0.606
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.613,
            0.484,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": "The velocity at a point is along the tangent to the circle at that point, in the direction of motion. A straight line that meets the circle at one and only point is called a tangent to the circle at that point (Fig. 4.25). You will learn more about it in Mathematics."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.538,
            0.609,
            0.7,
            0.689
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.538,
                0.692,
                0.699,
                0.721
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.25: Tangent to a circle at point B"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.748,
            0.407,
            0.903
          ],
          "content": "You know that acceleration is non-zero if the velocity of an object changes. The velocity changes if either its magnitude or direction, or both changes. In uniform circular motion, the motion of the object is accelerated because the direction of its velocity continuously changes."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.75,
            0.47,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.478,
            0.761,
            0.687,
            0.779
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.471,
            0.783,
            0.757,
            0.894
          ],
          "content": "Motion in space, such as a car climbing up a mountain road (Fig. 4.26), bird flying in the sky or an aircraft moving through air, is called motion in three dimensions. You will learn NextLevel about it in higher grades. **Up**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.7,
            0.852,
            0.749,
            0.89
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.802,
            0.082,
            0.856,
            0.098
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Note"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.8,
            0.102,
            0.944,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": "In uniform circular motion, the speed is constant at every point on the circle, it is only the direction of velocity that changes."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.781,
            0.267,
            0.952,
            0.394
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.793,
                0.4,
                0.941,
                0.429
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.24: A marble moving inside a ring"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_23.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.801,
            0.445,
            0.854,
            0.461
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Note"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.805,
            0.467,
            0.938,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "In everyday life, we say that a vehicle is accelerating when the magnitude of its velocity is changing but we often fail to recognise that there can be acceleration when there is only a change in the direction of velocity."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.761,
            0.779,
            0.943,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.757,
                0.879,
                0.948,
                0.894
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.26: A mountain road"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
